body:
_[Internal thread w/ context](https://dbt-labs.slack.com/archives/C091BNCDJ5T/p1757521922563829)_

**Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.**

In Fusion, we have "strict" validation of top-level configs:

https://docs.getdbt.com/reference/deprecations#customkeyinconfigdeprecation

That means users and package maintainers with custom configs must migrate them to nested fields within `meta`. They also need to update many macros/materializations.

We should give them utilities that make these updates as simple as find-replace.

**Is your feature request related to a particular component of the dbt Fusion Engine**
- [x] CLI Features
- [ ] Adapter or Database Driver Features
- [ ] SQL Understanding Features
- [ ] VS Code extension

**Describe the solution you'd like**

[Updated based on conversation below]

Two new context methods on the `config` object:
- `config.meta_get(<key_name>)`: Check `config.meta` for `key_name` first, and return if there is a value. ~If no value, then return `config.key_name`, which may be `None`.~
- `config.meta_require(<key_name>)`: Check `config.meta` for `key_name` first, and return if there is a value. ~If not found, check `config.key_name`.~ If no value, raise an error.

These need to be implemented in Fusion and [in dbt Core](https://github.com/dbt-labs/dbt-core/issues/12012), for back/forward compatibility during project migration.

This has come up with early adopters while migrating their custom configs to `meta`, since they "lose" some of the easy validation/enforcement they get with `config.require` - https://github.com/dbt-labs/dbt-core/issues/13606

**Describe alternatives you've considered**
Not doing this, requiring much trickier code changes within user projects / packages

**Additional context**

@b-per prepared an all-Jinja implementation:
```
{% macro config_meta_get(key, default=none) %}
    {%- if config.get(key) != none -%}
        {{ return(config.get(key)) }}
    {%- elif config.get("meta") != none and (key in config.get("meta", {}).keys()) -%}
        {{ return(config.get("meta").get(key)) }}
    {%- else -%}
        {{ return(default) }}
    {%- endif -%}
{% endmacro %}


{% macro config_meta_require(key) %}
    {# the first case is required to avoid errors #}
    {%- if config == {} -%}
        {{ return(none) }}
    {%- elif config.get(key) != none -%}
        {{ return(config.get(key)) }}
    {%- elif config.get("meta") != none and (key in config.get("meta", {}).keys()) -%}
        {{ return(config.get("meta").get(key)) }}
    {%- else -%}
        {% do exceptions.raise_compiler_error("Configuration '" ~ key ~ "' is required but was not found under config or meta (Fusion requires custom configuration under meta)") %}
    {%- endif -%}
{% endmacro %}
```

comments.body:
@will-sargent-dbtlabs Do you think it would be sufficient if `config.get()` and `config.require()` checked both top-level configs and values under meta?

The catch is that for anything pre-1.10, there is no concept of meta at all, and we are not shipping updates to those versions. So even if we make config.get() do the fallback in Fusion and Core 1.10+, we still have compatibility gaps for projects running older Core versions.

One option is to recommend that package maintainers ship their own `config_meta_get` and `config_meta_require` macros (like the ones below). Then anywhere they currently use config.get(), they can switch to these helpers. That gives them a single code path that works across pre-1.10 Core, 1.10, and Fusion.

```
{% macro config_meta_get(key, default=none) %}
    {%- if config.get(key) != none -%}
        {{ return(config.get(key)) }}
    {%- elif config.get("meta") != none and (key in config.get("meta", {}).keys()) -%}
        {{ return(config.get("meta").get(key)) }}
    {%- else -%}
        {{ return(default) }}
    {%- endif -%}
{% endmacro %}

{% macro config_meta_require(key) %}
    {%- if config == {} -%}
        {{ return(none) }}
    {%- elif config.get(key) != none -%}
        {{ return(config.get(key)) }}
    {%- elif config.get("meta") != none and (key in config.get("meta", {}).keys()) -%}
        {{ return(config.get("meta").get(key)) }}
    {%- else -%}
        {% do exceptions.raise_compiler_error("Configuration '" ~ key ~ "' is required but was not found under config or meta (Fusion requires custom configuration under meta)") %}
    {%- endif -%}
{% endmacro %}
```

This seems like a pragmatic way to give maintainers one reliable access pattern across all versions.


cc: @akbog @jtcohen6 @eliasdefaria

@venkaa28 For me: 
> Do you think it would be sufficient if config.get() and config.require() checked both top-level configs and values under meta?

It's enough for me. I think most (90%) of customers (not package maintainers) are going to have legacy code that is fixed by that single change.

My example:
```
{{ config ('
        materialized =  'table',
        my_custom_key= 'my_custom_value'
) }}

Refactored by autofix:
{{ config ('
        materialized = 'table',
        meta =  {
        'my_custom_key': 'my_custom_value'
        }
) }}
```

If config.get() and config.require() works for that refactor, that covers the biggest spread IMO.

That being said, I'd also want @b-per to weigh in on if that's good enough for the DX ecosystem (alongside suggested macro overrides if necessary)

The helpers are super cool. One of my challenges has been not understanding how to "override" the python jinja functions like config.get() for this problem.

@will-sargent-dbtlabs

Unfortunately, I don't think a list of "never fallback list" will solve the problem 😞 Consider my example [from above](https://github.com/dbt-labs/dbt-core/issues/13743#issuecomment-4597051423) would still be problematic. At step (1) and (2) `sub_materialization` isn't part of our maintained list of "never fallback configs". At step (3) we add `sub_materialization` to our list of "never fall back configs". At step (4) the user's project breaks because they're no longer getting the expected value.

I'd like to re-emphasis that commingling of user space vs engine space configs is _much more than a "non-zero" risk_ 😓 On the core team we have seen this cause problems multiple times. There have been _multiple high sev incidents_ caused by adding new top level configs which caused many threats of churn 😞
